---Settings controlling the volume of requests, connections and retries to this
---backend service.
---@class gcp.unstable.compute_beta.CircuitBreakers
---@field maxRequests? number The maximum number of parallel requests that allowed to the backend service. If not specified, there is no limit.
---@field maxRequestsPerConnection? number Maximum requests for a single connection to the backend service. This parameter is respected by both the HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 implementations. If not specified, there is no limit. Setting this parameter to 1 will effectively disable keep alive. Not supported when the backend service is referenced by a URL map that is bound to target gRPC proxy that has validateForProxyless field set to true.
---@field maxConnections? number The maximum number of connections to the backend service. If not specified, there is no limit. Not supported when the backend service is referenced by a URL map that is bound to target gRPC proxy that has validateForProxyless field set to true.
---@field connectTimeout? gcp.unstable.compute_beta.Duration The timeout for new network connections to hosts.
---@field maxRetries? number The maximum number of parallel retries allowed to the backend cluster. If not specified, the default is 1. Not supported when the backend service is referenced by a URL map that is bound to target gRPC proxy that has validateForProxyless field set to true.
---@field maxPendingRequests? number The maximum number of pending requests allowed to the backend service. If not specified, there is no limit. Not supported when the backend service is referenced by a URL map that is bound to target gRPC proxy that has validateForProxyless field set to true.

---@class gcp.unstable.compute_beta.CloneRulesFirewallPoliciesRequest
---@field firewallPolicy string Name of the firewall policy to update.
---@field sourceFirewallPolicy? string The firewall policy from which to copy rules.
---@field requestId? string An optional request ID to identify requests. Specify a unique request ID so that if you must retry your request, the server will know to ignore the request if it has already been completed. For example, consider a situation where you make an initial request and the request times out. If you make the request again with the same request ID, the server can check if original operation with the same request ID was received, and if so, will ignore the second request. This prevents clients from accidentally creating duplicate commitments. The request ID must be a valid UUID with the exception that zero UUID is not supported (00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000).

